WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.223 --> 00:00:01.890
KASLIN FIELDS: Hello,
I'm Kaslin Fields,

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:04.142
and I'm a developer
advocate at Google Cloud.

00:00:04.142 --> 00:00:05.850
Today, I'll be walking
through the basics

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:07.980
to get started with
Anthos on Bare Metal.

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:10.090
Let's get going.

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:12.460
Anthos is a platform
made up of a variety

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:15.850
of products all designed with
enterprise use cases in mind.

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:18.100
One key factor of
enterprise ecosystems

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:21.610
is that they often span hybrid
and multi cloud environments.

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:24.340
The Anthos platform is designed
to give a consistent user

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:26.410
experience across platforms.

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:29.110
You can expect that products
with Anthos in the name

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:31.090
will have features and
capabilities designed

00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:33.430
for hybrid and multi
cloud environments.

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:35.470
Today I'll be
talking about Anthos

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:37.510
on Bare Metal,
which is a tool you

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:39.970
can use to incorporate
your on-prem environments

00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:41.980
into the Anthos platform.

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:44.410
Anthos on Bare Metal gives
you the Google provided

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:47.200
consistent experience and
useful tools and features

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:51.790
of Anthos for you to run on
your own self-managed hardware.

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:53.960
We know that every
environment is different,

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:56.440
so there are a variety of
supported configurations, which

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:59.190
you can learn more about
in the documentation.

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:01.510
The install process with
Anthos on Bare Metal

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:04.000
includes an integrated
pre-flight check

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.640
to ensure your system
meets requirements.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:09.970
The installer itself allows you
to declaratively deploy Anthos

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:13.690
components onto your
own on-prem systems.

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:15.430
Anthos on Bare Metal
can be deployed

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:18.130
on either physical or
virtual infrastructure

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:20.950
and supports a variety
of Linux distributions,

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:25.420
such as Red Hat Enterprise
Linux, CentOS, or Ubuntu.

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:28.600
Anthos on Bare Metal comes with
an out-of-the-box Kubernetes

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:31.870
networking stack, including
load balancing and overlay

00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:32.800
networking.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:34.240
There are some
basic requirements

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:35.980
around networking for
the hardware you're

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:39.010
using to run your Anthos on
Bare Metal cluster, which

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:41.740
you can learn more about
in the documentation.

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:44.500
Integration with the
container storage interface

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:48.120
allows for integration with your
on-prem storage infrastructure.

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:51.280
Anthos on Bare Metal provides
tooling to perform smooth

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:55.030
in-place upgrades and includes
out-of-the-box integration with

00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:59.320
Cloud Operations, formerly
known as Stackdriver for System,

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:02.360
as well as application
logs and metrics,

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:04.420
as well as the potential
to integrate with other

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:06.310
observability tools.

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:09.100
While Anthos is largely
centered around Kubernetes,

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:12.940
we know not every workload may
be able to run in containers.

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:15.960
So Anthos can also
make use of Kubevirt.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:17.860
Kubevirt is an
open source project

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:20.410
allowing Kubernetes to
manage virtual machines

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:22.300
like it manages containers.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:23.860
There are a number
of prerequisites

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:25.930
your environment will need
to meet in order to be

00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:28.570
used with Anthos on Bare Metal.

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:31.210
Here, you can see a description
of the hardware environment

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:33.650
I'll be using in this demo.

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:37.240
I'll be using three Intel
Next Unit of Computing nodes,

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:39.070
or Intel NUCs.

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:44.380
I've imaged each of these to be
running Ubuntu 20.04.1 server.

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:46.003
There are a few
Linux distributions

00:02:46.003 --> 00:02:47.920
which meet the current
requirements for Anthos

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:49.240
on Bare Metal.

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:50.740
Be sure to check
the documentation

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:54.700
for the latest operating system
and hardware requirements.

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:59.830
I've upgraded to my NUCs to
have 32 gigabytes of RAM each.

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:02.410
These will be the nodes
where the Kubernetes cluster,

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:05.350
and, therefore, any
workloads, will be run.

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:09.350
The other NUC is to be used
as an admin workstation.

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:11.920
This can be any
Linux workstation.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:14.200
I'll use this node to
SSH into the others

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:18.640
to do any configuration and to
run the bmctl command, which

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:21.910
is the command line tool
for Anthos on Bare Metal.

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:24.250
There are also a number
of prerequisites to set up

00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:26.410
on the Google Cloud side.

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:28.810
You will need a project
and sufficient privileges

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:32.270
on that project to configure
Anthos on Bare Metal.

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:34.390
You will also need to
configure a few service

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:36.800
accounts for things like
logging and monitoring,

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:40.210
and to connect to your Bare
Metal cluster to Anthos in GCP.

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:41.710
Now let's take a
look at the process

00:03:41.710 --> 00:03:43.480
to set up an Anthos
on Bare Metal

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:46.000
cluster on this configuration.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.910
In order to create a cluster
for Anthos on Bare Metal,

00:03:48.910 --> 00:03:52.450
we'll need YAML file to
describe our Kubernetes cluster.

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:54.310
We can create a
convenient base file

00:03:54.310 --> 00:04:00.760
using the command bmctl
create config -c and giving it

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:03.045
a cluster name.

00:04:03.045 --> 00:04:04.420
There are several
components that

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:06.760
will need updating
in the created file.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:08.650
I've already created
one and configured it

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:10.278
for my environment.

00:04:10.278 --> 00:04:11.320
Let's take a closer look.

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:19.829
If you're familiar with
Kubernetes YAML manifests,

00:04:19.829 --> 00:04:23.000
this probably looks
familiar to you.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:25.160
At the top of this
file we have a variety

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:27.470
of key file paths,
which we will need

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:29.240
for a cluster installation.

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:32.850
Then we begin blocks
declaring API objects.

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:36.380
The first interesting one here
is the cluster definition.

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:40.290
You can see here that we
can define a cluster type.

00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:43.020
Anthos on Bare Metal
offers various architecture

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:45.840
configurations that can
be useful in multi cluster

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:48.530
environments, or a single
cluster environments,

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:49.455
such as at the edge.

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:55.280
We will use GKE
connect to register

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:56.485
this cluster with Anthos.

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:02.610
Here, we define the information
for our cluster's control

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:03.690
plane.

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:06.890
In my case, as this is
just a demo environment,

00:05:06.890 --> 00:05:09.930
I will be using a single
machine for my control plane.

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:13.150
For high availability, you
would generally want three.

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:17.850
Here, I have the IP my admin
workstation will use to connect

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:19.500
to the control plane node.

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:22.890
Next, I need to specify the
IP ranges Kubernetes will use

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:25.290
to create pods and services.

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:28.050
There is also a load balancer
component needed for Anthos

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:29.310
on Bare Metal.

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:31.410
This can run on the
control plane nodes,

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:34.740
or as a separate machine
or set of machines.

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:37.800
In my demo environment, I will
be using my control plane node

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:40.290
also as my load
balancer, so here

00:05:40.290 --> 00:05:42.990
I specify a virtual
IP on my network

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:45.360
to be used for
the load balancer.

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:48.930
I also specify a range of IPs
for use by the load balancer

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:50.970
to expose my services.

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:52.710
If you're using a
proxy, you'll need

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:54.930
to add some configuration here.

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:57.510
I'm not, so I'll leave
this all commented out.

00:05:57.510 --> 00:06:01.230
Here, we see some information
used for logging and metrics.

00:06:01.230 --> 00:06:02.760
And here at the
bottom of the file

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:05.820
is the definition of
our worker node pool.

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:08.910
The machines in this pool are
where workloads will actually

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:10.020
be run.

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:12.600
As I mentioned, in my
case, I have only one.

00:06:16.950 --> 00:06:20.460
Now I'm going to use bmctl, the
Anthos Bare Metal command line

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:23.190
tool, to deploy the
cluster I've defined.

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:30.930
To do that, I'll run
bmctl create cluster -c

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:33.600
and give it a cluster name.

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:36.450
This will take a few
minutes to complete.

00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:40.140
Now we've successfully
deployed our cluster.

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:41.610
We can confirm
that we can connect

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:43.920
to our cluster using kubectl.

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:46.530
We can see in the output
of our cluster creation

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:48.507
the Kubeconfig file
we'll need to use

00:06:48.507 --> 00:06:49.590
to connect to the cluster.

00:06:53.390 --> 00:07:00.440
Running this command
shows me the nodes

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:02.600
of my two-node demo cluster.

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:05.540
I'll also go ahead and
export my Kubeconfig

00:07:05.540 --> 00:07:07.700
so I don't have to specify
it in each command.

00:07:12.110 --> 00:07:15.680
The Bare Metal command line tool
not only created the cluster,

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:19.430
but also connected the cluster
to Anthos in Google Cloud,

00:07:19.430 --> 00:07:22.040
so we should be able to see
our cluster in the Anthos

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:24.725
dashboard in the Google
Cloud console, as well.

00:07:28.210 --> 00:07:29.890
Here in the Google
Cloud console,

00:07:29.890 --> 00:07:31.810
we will go to the
Anthos dashboard.

00:07:37.550 --> 00:07:39.350
And here in the
Anthos dashboard,

00:07:39.350 --> 00:07:41.960
you can see that, in
the cluster status box,

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:45.060
it lists one healthy cluster.

00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:49.130
If we click View Clusters,
we can see our cluster here.

00:07:49.130 --> 00:07:50.810
This is the cluster
we just created

00:07:50.810 --> 00:07:53.480
on a set of Intel NUC hardware.

00:07:53.480 --> 00:07:57.530
And now we can see and
manage it in Google Cloud.

00:07:57.530 --> 00:08:00.470
Now let's deploy an
app to our cluster.

00:08:00.470 --> 00:08:04.850
For this demo, I'm going to use
a very simple Hello World app.

00:08:04.850 --> 00:08:09.860
If we look at our
deployment YAML file,

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:13.130
you can see that I'm defining
a Kubernetes deployment here

00:08:13.130 --> 00:08:17.210
with three replicas and using
a Hello app container image.

00:08:23.190 --> 00:08:24.770
Now, to create my
deployment, I'll

00:08:24.770 --> 00:08:30.640
run kubectl create -f and
give it my deployment file.

00:08:36.270 --> 00:08:39.250
You can see that the
deployment has been created.

00:08:39.250 --> 00:08:45.210
Now we can check it
with kubectl get deploy.

00:08:45.210 --> 00:08:49.140
You can see our deployment
is up with three ready pods.

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:52.830
Now I'll create a service
using the my-service.yaml file.

00:08:56.110 --> 00:08:58.840
You can see that this is
a service of type load

00:08:58.840 --> 00:09:02.040
balancer, which we'll be
able to reach on port 80.

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:09.280
We'll create this with
kubectl create -f and give it

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:11.140
the file name.

00:09:11.140 --> 00:09:14.260
And we can take a look at
our service with kubectl

00:09:14.260 --> 00:09:16.780
get svc, or service.

00:09:16.780 --> 00:09:21.130
Using my service's external IP
provided by the load balancer,

00:09:21.130 --> 00:09:23.050
I can run a curl
command on that IP

00:09:23.050 --> 00:09:26.400
to see that my app
is running properly.

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:28.920
Here you can see in our
output that my application

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:31.200
is running as I expect.

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:33.360
In this demo, we walked
through the requirements

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:35.670
to create an Anthos
on Bare Metal Cluster,

00:09:35.670 --> 00:09:38.970
saw some of the benefits you
get from Anthos in Google Cloud,

00:09:38.970 --> 00:09:42.350
and deployed an application
to our on-prem cluster.

00:09:42.350 --> 00:09:44.280
Check out Google Cloud
for more on running

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:46.500
enterprise-grade,
hybrid, and multi cloud

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:48.780
architectures with Anthos.

